\begin{align*}
&\text{All men are mortal.} \\
&\text{Socrates is a man.} \\
&\therefore \text{Socrates is mortal.}
\end{align*}

In this example, the first proposition (premise) asserts that all men are mortal. The second proposition (another premise) states that Socrates is a man. From these two premises, we can logically infer that Socrates is mortal. This conclusion follows because the term "men" includes Socrates since he is one, and the property of being mortal applies to all men by the first premise.

