response:
 To push the variable into the registrations state after making an API call in `checkMNPStatusAPI` function, you need to use the functional setState instead of directly mutating the state object. Here's how you can modify your code to achieve this:

First, make sure to bind the `this` context to the `checkMNPStatusAPI` function in the constructor or using arrow functions.

```javascript
constructor(props) {
  super(props);
  this.state = {
    registrations: []
  };
  this.checkMNPStatusAPI = this.checkMNPStatusAPI.bind(this);
}
```

Then, update the `checkMNPStatusAPI` function to use functional setState:

```javascript
checkMNPStatusAPI(phoneNo, index) {
  console.log("mnpphoneno: " + phoneNo);

  var headers = new Headers();
  headers.append("Authorization", "Bearer " + user.getAccessToken());

  fetch(api.checkPortInStatus(user.getProductCode(), phoneNo), {
    headers: headers,
    method: 'GET'
  })
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(rjson => {
    // Create a copy of the registrations array
    let updatedRegistrations = [...this.state.registrations];

    // Update the specific registration object at given index
    updatedRegistrations[index].mnpStatus = rjson.status;

    // Set the updated state
    this.setState({ registrations: updatedRegistrations });
  })
}
```

This way, when the state is updated with `this.setState`, React will schedule a re-render automatically for you, ensuring that the changes made to the state are reflected on the UI.
